From 9ce0e076f5a10934b3791fb8f80fc9900e6a7ceb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ss3ds <ss3ds@localhost>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:59:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ss3ds: 8-bit reads/writes for 16-bit IO registers (Ditto
 Transform MOSAIC crash)

A wild Ditto using Transform halted the game with:

[FATAL] translated assembly 8-bit write of unmapped DS register 0400004d

0x0400004d is the high byte of the 16-bit MOSAIC register at 0x0400004c (OBJ mosaic size lives in the high byte, which Transform's animation writes). ds_ioreg.c only emitted 8-bit accessors for the genuinely 8-bit VRAMCNT registers, so any translated-assembly byte access to a 16-bit register aborted.

Add byte-half accessors for every 16-bit register in the read16/write16 tables: a byte read returns the low/high byte, a byte write updates only that byte, exactly as the memory-mapped hardware behaves. The 16-bit and 32-bit paths are untouched.

Excluded, deliberately: the DISP_MMEM_FIFO halves (FIFO data, not stored state), the memory-card interface (MCCNT0/MCD0), the IPC registers (SUBPINTF/SUBP_FIFO_CNT), and VRAM_HI_CNT at 0x04000248, whose bytes are already the VRAMCNT_H/I 8-bit cases. Byte accesses to 32-bit registers still abort loudly, as before.

Note: ds_ioreg.c is generated by gen_ioreg.py, which only emits width-exact cases; if it is ever re-run, the same byte cases must be re-derived or this fix is lost. The build compiles ds_ioreg.c directly and does not regenerate it.
